    26 November 2025 

\vspace{10pt}

    Johnny Cade: A Story of Choice Versus Destiny 

\vspace{10pt}

    Some things in life are simply out of one’s control, such as the family they were born 

\vspace{10pt}

    into, or the surroundings they grew up in. While this is the case, everyone and anyone still has 

\vspace{10pt}

    the power to decide how they act within these circumstances. William Ernest Henley’s poem 

\vspace{10pt}

    “Invictus” deeply explores this idea of choice versus destiny. Similarly in the novel The 

\vspace{10pt}

    Outsiders by S.E. Hinton, Johnny Cade is a Greaser who seems to have won the lottery when it 

\vspace{10pt}

    comes to bad luck. The scrawny 16 year old yearns for a world of love, yet is neglected by his 

\vspace{10pt}

    parents and constantly targeted by a rival gang. Apart from a few friends, Johnny is left to deal 

\vspace{10pt}

    with the hardships of life all on his own. Through its theme of “one has the power to shape their 

\vspace{10pt}

    future, regardless of their current state”, the poem “Invictus” perfectly encapsulates the character 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/pTkr478/localstore/outsiders-final-essay-google-docsassets/3.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    In Hinton's novel The Outsiders, life throws many misfortunes at Johnny, many of which 

\vspace{10pt}

    others don’t have to deal with. In the beginning of the novel, Ponyboy introduces Johnny along 

\vspace{10pt}

    with the other Greasers, explaining, “If you can picture a little dark puppy that has been kicked 

\vspace{10pt}

    too many times and is lost in a crowd of strangers, you'll have Johnny...He had a nervous, 

\vspace{10pt}

    suspicious look in his eyes, and that beating he got from the Socs didn’t help matters. He was the 

\vspace{10pt}

    gang’s pet, everyone’s kid brother. His father was always beating him up, and his mother ignored 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    him” (Hinton 11-12). In this description, Johnny is portrayed as a helpless boy who has no 

\vspace{10pt}

    choice but to accept all the adversity in life. None of the challenges he is forced to face were a 

\vspace{10pt}

    result of his own actions; after all, everyone is innocent when they are born. To make the 

\vspace{10pt}

    situation worse, Johnny has little support and love in the world except from a few of the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Greasers. All in all, Johnny feels powerless when it comes to conquering his fears and achieving 

\vspace{10pt}

    what he really wants: a life in a world of love. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Even so, Johnny develops into a more confident and independent character later in the 

\vspace{10pt}

    novel. One example of this is when he realizes that children were trapped inside a burning 

\vspace{10pt}

    church. Ponyboy recalls Johnny risking his life to rescue them, describing, “One was screaming 

\vspace{10pt}

    his head off, and Johnny yelled, ‘Shut up! We’re goin’ to get you out!’ The kid looked surprised 

\vspace{10pt}

    and quit hollering. I blinked myself— Johnny wasn’t behaving at all like his old self...He wasn’t 

\vspace{10pt}

    scared either. That was the only time I can think of when I saw him without that defeated, 

\vspace{10pt}

    suspicious look in his eyes. He looked like he was having the time of his life” (Hinton 92). Here, 

\vspace{10pt}

    Johnny’s actions reflect the major growth he experiences throughout the story. Whereas the old 

\vspace{10pt}

    Johnny would have been timid, the new Johnny not only triumphs over his own past, but fights 

\vspace{10pt}

    for the future of others as well. As seen throughout the novel The Outsiders, Johnny’s character 

\vspace{10pt}

    strongly embodies the lesson of taking control of one’s destiny regardless of their past adversity. 

\vspace{10pt}

    A similar theme is explored in the poem “Invictus” by William Ernest Henley. The 

\vspace{10pt}

    speaker is resilient as he declares, “Under the bludgeonings of chance/My head is bloody, but 

\vspace{10pt}

    unbowed” (Henley 7-8). The “bludgeonings of chance” represent the many random misfortunes 

\vspace{10pt}

    and hardships that the speaker has gone through in life. The speaker could have chosen to 

\vspace{10pt}

    complain about the unfairness of life and give in, but he endures through the pain, keeping his 

\vspace{10pt}

    head held up. Many of these ideas correspond to Johnny’s life from The Outsiders. Combined 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    with a brilliant use of alliteration, these lines express that the speaker is resilient through the 

\vspace{10pt}

    rough side of life. 

\vspace{10pt}

    The speaker further emphasizes this point later in the poem. In the last four lines, the 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/pTkr478/localstore/outsiders-final-essay-google-docsassets/4.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    It matters not how strait the gate, 

\vspace{10pt}

    How charged with punishments the scroll, 

\vspace{10pt}

    I am the master of my fate: 

\vspace{10pt}

    I am the captain of my soul (Henley 13-16). 

\vspace{10pt}

    Announcing that his bloodstained past no longer matters, the speaker declares that just 

\vspace{10pt}

    because life has not been the most fair to him, it doesn’t mean he won’t keep going. These lines 

\vspace{10pt}

    very much reflect Johnny when he ends his life of victimhood to take his last breath as a hero. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Although his ending was short-lived, Johnny nonetheless triumphs over his hardships as he 

\vspace{10pt}

    decides to take control of his own destiny. In both the novel and poem, the main character 

\vspace{10pt}

    prevails over their past and eventually decides their own fate. 

\vspace{10pt}

    In conclusion, Johnny’s character from The Outsiders is clearly reflected in the poem 

\vspace{10pt}

    “Invictus”. Both Johnny and the speaker grapple with the choice of adhering to their misfortunes 

\vspace{10pt}

    in life and fighting for a future that they desire. Not everyone is born into a neglectful family, nor 

\vspace{10pt}

    is everyone forced to constantly fear being jumped by a rival gang. Nevertheless, given these 

\vspace{10pt}

    unfortunate circumstances, Johnny ultimately decides to take charge of his fate and fight for 

\vspace{10pt}

    himself and those around him. Every person’s life belongs to them and them only; like Johnny 

\vspace{10pt}

    and the speaker from “Invictus”, everyone possesses the power to shape their own destiny. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

